`,s.jsx(M,{items:[{q:"Expand SARIMA(1,0,0)(1,0,0)_12 into an explicit equation. How many lags appear, and how many parameters were typed?",a:"y_t = phi*y_{t-1} + Phi*y_{t-12} - phi*Phi*y_{t-13} + eps_t. Three lags appear from two typed parameters, because the seasonal block multiplies rather than adds — the lag-13 cross term's coefficient is forced to be minus the product."},{q:"What does the I in ARIMA actually do to the prediction interval, not just the point forecast?",a:"Integrating the forecast back to the original scale cumulatively sums the psi-weights, so the forecast-error variance accumulates far faster than in a stationary ARMA. That is why an ARIMA band fans out steeply with horizon — and why hand-differencing without re-integrating the variance produces forecasts that look absurdly confident."},{q:"Two SARIMA fits report AIC 340 (d=1, D=1) and AIC 295 (d=0, D=1). Which wins?",a:"Neither — the comparison is meaningless. AIC is only comparable across models fit to the same differenced data, and changing d changes the series the likelihood is computed on. Fix d and D by stationarity testing FIRST, then use AIC only to choose p, q, P, Q."},{q:"Why is a HIGH Ljung-Box p-value on the residuals the good outcome?",a:"Its null hypothesis is that the residuals are independent — white noise, nothing left to model. Failing to reject means the model has extracted the structure. A low p-value says autocorrelation survives in the residuals, so there is signal you have not captured and the intervals are too narrow. Same inverted logic as ADF."},{q:"Give the order in which you difference a trending seasonal series, and the reason.",a:"Seasonal difference first (D), then check whether a regular difference (d) is still needed. Seasonal differencing at lag m often removes the trend as a side effect, so doing it first frequently saves a difference — and every extra difference costs observations and adds noise."}]}),`
`,s.jsx(D,{question:"SARIMA(1,0,0)(1,0,0)₁₂ is fit to monthly data. Which lags of y appear in the expanded equation?",options:["Lags 1 and 12 only — one term per typed parameter","Lags 1, 12 and 13 — the multiplicative form generates a cross-term at lag 13","All lags from 1 to 12","Lag 12 only; the non-seasonal part applies to the residuals"],correct:1}),`
`,s.jsx(D,{question:"A SARIMA model's residual Ljung–Box test returns p = 0.003 at lag 12. What is the right next step?",options:["Ship it — a low p-value confirms the residuals are white noise","Increase the forecast horizon to average out the effect","Revisit the seasonal terms — autocorrelation survives in the residuals, so the model is misspecified","Switch to a confidence interval instead of a prediction interval"],correct:2}),`
